# Line-ending policy, checked in.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# The Linux build (build_linux.sh, package_linux.sh) runs on the ubuntu
# runner and fails instantly with `/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter` if git
# checks the scripts out with CRLF endings - which is exactly what happens
# by default on a Windows working copy, since core.autocrlf is usually
# true. Declaring the endings here makes the checked-out bytes independent
# of the machine that cloned, so a Linux build never fails because the
# developer who last committed was on Windows.

# Shell scripts: LF everywhere, including on a Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# Windows batch/PowerShell scripts are read by tools that want CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binaries are never converted.
*.exe binary
*.AppImage binary
*.deb binary
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.db binary
